        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Jackery Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick by the heaviest load you need to run and how often you will move the station. Jackery's lineup has a clean answer for almost every combination, and the most expensive model is the right one for very few people.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phones, laptops, lights and a CPAP</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Explorer 300 Plus</strong>. At about 8 pounds it is the easiest Jackery to take anywhere, and its 288Wh battery covers a night of CPAP use with the humidifier off or a long day of device charging. If you need to run a small appliance like a blender or a portable induction plate, skip past it; 300 watts is not enough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weekend camping and short outages</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Explorer 1000 v2</strong>. It is the lightest 1kWh LFP box from a major brand, it starts a refrigerator, and it charges from the wall in about an hour in its app-enabled emergency mode. For a household that wants to keep the fridge cold and the Wi-Fi on through an evening outage, it is the simplest good answer. Campers should read our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power station for camping</a> for how it compares against rivals at the campsite.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">RVs, vans and overnight outages</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Explorer 2000 v2</strong>. Two kilowatt-hours in a 40-pound box covers a fridge for about a day or a full night of CPAP, fridge, router and lights. RV owners comparing it with a house battery upgrade should also read our guide to the <a href="/best-lithium-rv-battery/">best lithium RV battery</a>, because a built-in battery bank may suit a full-time rig better than a portable box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Growing toward home backup</h3>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Explorer 1000 Plus</strong> if you want to start small and stay portable, or the <strong>Explorer 2000 Plus</strong> if you already know you want circuit-level backup and possibly 240V. Only a planned whole-home installation with a transfer switch justifies the <strong>Explorer 5000 Plus</strong> or a HomePower model, and at that budget you should compare it with the options in our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="v2-vs-plus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Explorer v2 or Explorer Plus?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The v2 and Plus lines overlap at 1kWh and 2kWh, and the choice between them is really a choice about the future. Compare the Explorer 1000 v2 and Explorer 1000 Plus: Jackery lists the v2 at 1,070Wh and 1,500W in about 24 pounds, and the Plus at 1,264Wh and 2,000W in about 32 pounds. The Plus has more capacity and more output, but it is a third heavier and costs more.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the extra weight buys is the expansion port. The 1000 Plus takes up to three battery packs for about 5kWh in total; the 1000 v2 takes none. If there is any chance you will want more than 1kWh, the Plus avoids buying a second station later. If you are certain 1kWh is your ceiling, the v2 is the better object to own: lighter, cheaper and simpler.
          </p>

          <p>
            At 2kWh the gap widens. The 2000 v2 and 2000 Plus store the same 2,042Wh by Jackery's figures, but the Plus has a 3,000W inverter against the v2's 2,200W and weighs about 61 pounds against about 40. The 2000 Plus also links with a second unit for 120/240V, which the v2 never will. For a household building a home system, the Plus is the right platform. For an RV owner who needs 2kWh and wants to lift it into the rig, the v2 is far more practical.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple test helps. Write down the biggest load you need, the longest outage you plan for, and whether the station will be carried or rolled. If your list stops at a refrigerator and one night, buy a v2. If it includes a well pump, a furnace, several days or the word 240V, buy a Plus and plan the expansion from the start.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">v2 if you know the size; Plus if you might grow.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Jackery's v2 boxes are the lighter, simpler buy at a fixed capacity. The Plus line costs more and weighs more, but it is the only Jackery path to more storage and 240V.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Home Backup: Plus Systems, HomePower and Transfer Switches
          </h2>

          <p>
            Any Jackery can back up a house the simple way, with extension cords from the station to the refrigerator, the router and a few lamps. That needs no installation and works through most outages. What it cannot do is run hardwired loads such as a furnace blower, a well pump or anything on a 240V circuit, because those cannot be plugged into the front of a power station.
          </p>

          <p>
            For those, you need a station that can feed the house panel through a transfer switch or an interlock with a generator inlet, installed by a licensed electrician. In Jackery's range, that starts with the larger Plus systems. Two linked Explorer 2000 Plus units provide 120/240V, and the Explorer 5000 Plus delivers it from a single box. Jackery sells its own transfer switch for these larger systems; check that the switch and station you are buying are listed as compatible before ordering either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jackery's HomePower line, including the HomePower 3600 Plus, is the brand's newer push into dedicated home backup. These units are designed to sit in one place and connect to the house rather than travel. Because specifications and availability in this line have changed recently, we have not ranked a HomePower model here; if you are shopping for one, compare its published capacity, continuous output and 240V capability directly against the 2000 Plus and 5000 Plus before you decide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never try to power the house by plugging a station into a wall or dryer outlet with a male-to-male cord. It is illegal, it can push power back onto the grid and injure a utility worker, and it can start a fire. If you want circuits rather than cords, pay for the transfer switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a wider view of the options, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power stations for home backup</a> guide sets Jackery's large units against EcoFlow's Delta Pro 3 and Anker's SOLIX F3800, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">best home battery backup</a> systems covers wall-mounted batteries that compete with the 5000 Plus on price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Jackery Solar Generators and SolarSaga Panels
          </h2>

          <p>
            A Jackery solar generator is not a separate product. It is an Explorer power station sold together with one or more of Jackery's SolarSaga folding panels and the cable to connect them, and the station inside is identical to the one sold alone. Jackery leans on the term more than most brands, so it is worth reading bundle listings carefully: the name often tells you the station model and the number of panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            The question to ask of any bundle is whether the panels can refill the battery in a day. A good rule of thumb is one watt of panel for every three watt-hours you want back, because a folding panel rarely delivers its full rating for more than a few hours. By that measure, one 100W panel suits the Explorer 300 Plus, a single 200W panel tops up a 1000 v2 at a campsite where you arrive full, and two or three 200W panels are what it takes to cycle a 2000 v2 daily during an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Single-panel bundles for the larger stations are the most common disappointment. A 2000 v2 with one 200W panel will put back perhaps a third of its capacity on a clear day, which is fine for topping up between campsites but not for living off solar through a week without power. If you plan to depend on solar, buy the bundle with more panels or add panels separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            SolarSaga panels pair cleanly with every Explorer, but they are not the only option. Third-party panels work as long as the connector, voltage and current fall within the station's published solar input range. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> works through the sizing math for each station class, and our roundup of the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> covers folding panels that fit Jackery inputs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="app-charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Charging Speed, the App and Battery Protection
          </h2>

          <p>
            For years Jackery trailed EcoFlow on wall charging speed, and the v2 generation largely closed that gap. Jackery quotes about an hour from empty to full on the Explorer 1000 v2 in its emergency charging mode. The catch is that the fast mode is switched on through the Jackery app; out of the box, the station charges at a gentler default rate that Jackery positions as better for long-term battery health.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a reasonable design. Most owners do not need a one-hour charge most of the time, and slower charging is easier on the cells. But if you are buying for a storm season with rolling blackouts, install the app before the storm and learn where the setting is, because an hour of grid power is only useful if the station can absorb it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jackery's app is simpler than EcoFlow's, and the stations are designed to work without it. It shows input and output watts, sets charge modes and battery limits, and toggles outputs. Jackery also markets a battery management system it calls ChargeShield, which adjusts charging behavior to protect the cells. For a parent or grandparent who will never open an app, that simplicity is a real advantage: the 1000 v2 is about as close to plug-and-forget as a power station gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jackery's v2 and Plus units can also act as a basic battery backup for a router or modem when left plugged in, switching over quickly when the grid drops. For a desktop computer or a CPAP that must not blink, check the switchover figure on the specific model's spec sheet, and consider a dedicated UPS for equipment that cannot tolerate any interruption. Our guide to the <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">best battery backup for CPAP</a> covers the CPAP case in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-jackery-loses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Where Jackery Loses (and What Not to Buy)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Jackery's strengths are weight and simplicity. Its weaknesses are the flip side. The v2 models do not expand, so a 1000 v2 that turns out to be too small has to be replaced or supplemented rather than upgraded. EcoFlow's Delta 3 Plus, by contrast, takes add-on batteries at the same size, which is why our <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">EcoFlow power station guide</a> ranks it as the more flexible 1kWh buy for home use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Output per size class is the other gap. At 1kWh, the 1000 v2's 1,500W continuous rating sits below the 1,800W that EcoFlow and Bluetti offer, and many kettles, toasters and hair dryers draw 1,500 to 1,800W, right at or past the Jackery's limit. If those appliances are on your list, the 1000 Plus or a rival is the better choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip older Jackery models still sold through third-party listings, such as the original Explorer 1000 and 1500 with NMC batteries, unless the discount is deep and the use is occasional. They are heavier per watt-hour, charge more slowly and wear out sooner than the LFP models above. And treat the 5000 Plus as a home battery, not a portable power station: if you will not install a transfer switch, two 2000 Plus units or a 2000 v2 cover most needs for much less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, for the longest outages, a battery may not be the cheapest answer at all. A good inverter generator delivers more energy per dollar for multi-day use, and our guides to the <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generators</a> and the <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">best generator for camping</a> cover the fuel-burning alternatives honestly, noise and fumes included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Each Jackery Will Run Your Gear
          </h2>

          <p>
            A station never delivers its full rated capacity as AC power. Inverter losses and battery management typically leave something like 80 to 90 percent of the label usable, so plan with about 85 percent and you will be close. On that basis the Explorer 300 Plus gives you around 245Wh, the 1000 v2 a little over 900Wh, the 2000 v2 roughly 1,700Wh and the 5000 Plus somewhere above 4,000Wh.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a CPAP without the heated humidifier, drawing about 30 to 60 watts, the 300 Plus covers about one night and the 1000 v2 several. The humidifier can double or triple consumption, which is the usual reason a small station runs flat before dawn. For a refrigerator using 1 to 2kWh a day, the 1000 v2 covers most of a day, the 2000 v2 about a day, and the 5000 Plus several days if nothing else is running.
          </p>

          <p>
            For electronics, even the smallest Jackery goes a long way. A modem and router together draw around 15 to 30 watts, so the 300 Plus keeps a home office online for most of a working day. Phones barely register. If devices are your only concern, a large power bank may do the job; our roundup of <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> covers lighter, cheaper options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add loads and the hours shrink fast. A chest freezer, a furnace blower and a few lights alongside the fridge can double daily consumption, which is exactly the point where the expandable Plus models earn their extra weight. Build your load list before you buy, add 20 percent for the things you forgot, and choose the station that covers it with margin.
          </p>
        </div>

