            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a v2 when you will want more later</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Explorer 1000 v2 and 2000 v2 cannot take add-on batteries. If there is a real chance you will want home backup, more runtime or 240V down the road, start with an Explorer Plus model instead. Replacing a fixed-size station later costs more than buying the expandable one now.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the 1000 v2 to run a kettle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery rates the 1000 v2 at 1,500W continuous, and many kettles, toasters and hair dryers draw 1,500 to 1,800W. Some will trip the station's overload protection. If heating appliances are on your list, look at the 1000 Plus with 2,000W or a 2kWh model instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a one-panel bundle to refill a big station</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Jackery solar generator bundle with a single 200W panel will put back only a fraction of a 2kWh battery on a clear day. That suits topping up while camping, not living on solar through an outage. Size panels at roughly one watt per three watt-hours you need back.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never turning on fast charging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jackery's quickest wall charge is enabled through the app, and the station defaults to a slower rate. In a storm with rolling outages, that difference decides whether the battery is full by nightfall. Install the app and set the charge mode before the season starts, not during the blackout.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Powering the house through an outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connecting a station to a wall or dryer outlet with a double-male cord to energize the house is illegal and dangerous to utility workers. Run extension cords to individual appliances, or have an electrician install a transfer switch for a 2000 Plus pair or a 5000 Plus.
              </p>
            </div>
